# Models, Migrations & Seeding

## Models

- One Mongoose model per PascalCase file; default-export the model.
- Always set `{ timestamps: true }`.
- Use schema field options (`required`, `trim`, `lowercase`, `enum`, `default`).
- Put side effects in pre-save hooks (e.g. password hashing) and instance methods on the schema.

```js
const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true, lowercase: true, trim: true },
  role: { type: String, enum: ['admin', 'author'], default: 'author' },
}, { timestamps: true })

export default mongoose.model('User', userSchema)
```

## Migrations

- Schema/index/collection changes go through migrate-mongo, not ad-hoc scripts.
- Create with `npm run migrate:create`; files are named `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS-description.js`.
- Export `up(db)` / `down(db)` using the raw MongoDB driver (`db.collection(...)`), and log via `dbLogger`.

## Seeding

- Seed data lives in `fixtures/`; the seeder is [server/scripts/seed.js](server/scripts/seed.js) (`npm run seed`).
- Let model hooks run (e.g. create users via `User.create` so passwords hash); log operations with `dbLogger`.
